Z||000004||ChadMan||07-12-2000||08:40 AM||||Sounds like a bargain (especially to someone who just paid $450 for 4 new 1988 calipers), but I wonder about two things:<P>1)  How will your front brakes and back brakes "get along" after the conversion?  Will your fronts lock up way early?<P>2)  Will you be able to get replacement pads anywhere other than the Fiero Store?  Or does this conversion share pads with other GM cars?||||reg||1
Z||000005||BBBAD GT||07-12-2000||09:37 AM||||Not bad at all, cept I did all four wheels (GA stuff) for roughly $300 (carparts 33% off deal tho), and $50 more for the blazer MC.  I think the GA caliper is 5mm bigger than the stock Fiero, I could be wrong tho.  Seems like a winner if it is indeed better than the popular GA upgrade.<P><P>------------------<BR>87 GT, Stock for now<BR><A HREF="http://scribers.midwest.net/bbbad50/Fiero.htm" TARGET=_blank>BBBAD GT's Web Site</A>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Gold-86SE||07-12-2000||10:36 AM||||<BR>I share chadman's concern about the brake pads. Can we use normal off-the-shelf pads as replacements? If so, man, what a deal.<P>Makes me wish I had 300 bucks today to order the kit.<P>Laters<P><P>------------------<BR>Gold-86SE (Fiero Collector)||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||07-12-2000||11:12 AM||||I called the Fiero Store and the pads and rotors are "off the shelf" GM parts. (92 Baretta) The rotors and pads are slightly bigger than the 91 and earlier GA/Baretta brakes. That is why you need the adapter for the caliper mounting. The only part that is "specially made" is the hub assembly.<BR>Now if they would make a rear caliper that used the GA rotors and had the E-brake hooked up.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Fret Naught||07-12-2000||12:35 PM||||I don't know, I am still thinking about going to slotted rotors, same size, with pads it works out to be about 100.00 per wheel, and I have heard great reports on their performance, ||||reg||5
Z||000009||Monkeyman||07-12-2000||12:45 PM||||Guess I should have read the rest of the posts before making a long distance call to the Fiero Store.<P>They said that there are <B>NO</B> modifications required.  This means you don't have to grind anything off.  All Autozone stock parts other than the mounting brackets and hubs.  I'd be willing to bet that you could do the Beretta/GA upgrade to the back and have the entire thing done for less than you could by doing it yourself.  I think I'll do mine this way.  I was mighty scared to think that I'd have to grind something.<P>Oreif--Thanks for posting this.  Maybe now I'll have decent brakes by the middle of August (taking a road trip then).<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Oreif||07-12-2000||01:56 PM||||Long distance call?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/eek.gif">  I just called 1-800-FIERO GT (1-800-343-7648) You can call the toll free number and still get technical questions answered.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/LOGO3.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000011||Monkeyman||07-12-2000||02:48 PM||||It figures.  I saw the disclaimer (?) so I dialed up the long distance #.  Anyway, I'll probably order it Thursday or Friday.  Hopefully it'll get here quickly.  Any idea what shipping would cost to mail the core back from Indiana?  Just trying to figure out if it would be worth the core charge.<P>Hopefully I can install the front brakes in a couple of hours.  I guess the rear brakes would be even easier, right?<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||07-12-2000||05:46 PM||||I installed that kit on my brothers 87 fiero coupe from the Fiero Store. The parts seem to be the cheapest they could find. The rotors don't have the correct surface on them so you have to get them turned. The caliper slides don't have any grease in them and I had trouble getting them out to grease them. The brakes work well except he has more pedal travel and the pads rattle a lot over bumps like there is missing rattle clips. If I were to do it all over again I would just replace the stock brakes. One more thing, the calipers are cast iron so they weight a lot more than the stock fiero calipers.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Monkeyman||07-13-2000||01:03 PM||||The Blazer MC should take care of some of that extra pedal travel.  As far as the rattling pads go, they make a shim kit for most brakes.  Maybe that would help.  Everything other than the mounting bracket and machined hub is supposed to be stock, buy-it-at-your-local-Autozone parts for an '88 Grand Am.  I don't know why there would be any problems with it.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
Z||000014||mrfixit58||07-13-2000||04:41 PM||||Monkeyman,<P>I have an idea for you if your worried about the grinding part. Do you still have the Monkeymobile? If so, use it for practice. Just go down to your local parts store, buy the caliper, grind the bracket until the caliper fits. <P>If you are sucessful, you'll gain the confidence you need to tackle the Grapeape. If you mess it up and decide that the kit is the better way to go, just return the caliper and get your money back. <P>Roy  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>||||reg||1
Z||000015||BAM-BAM||07-14-2000||07:42 AM||||  I believe that Randy Agee said the Baretta callipers were a full 10mm larger tht the Fiero calipers. That would provide better stopping power than the Fiero Store's kit, wouldn't it? MonkeyMan, The grinding is really simple and nothing to worry about. Just follow Randy's instructions. <A HREF="http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/caliperpistons/index.html" TARGET=_blank>http://fierozone.tripod.com/realbrakes/caliperpistons/index.html</A> <P>  Late, BAM OUT! D:<BR>||||reg||12
Z||000016||Monkeyman||07-14-2000||12:29 PM||||I'm sure I could do the grinding, but I need to get it done by the middle of next month (road trip) and, frankly, I'm slightly terrified of trashing either of my cars.  (Yes, I still have the Monkeymobile, but it's a little hard to get to--it's in a storage room)  Just because I can get it right on the Monkeymobile, doesn't necessarily mean that I'll get it right on the Grape Ape.  I'll just have to cough up the cash (wouldn't it be nice if it actually worked that way-hack  hack   hack) for the Fiero Stores stuff.<P>------------------<BR>Dave-<BR>Grape Ape ('85GT)||||reg||1
